{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for severe mean reversion lag: The model consistently underestimates the depth of immediate price drops (e.g., 79 to 70) and overestimates the speed of recovery, failing to capture the V-shaped volatility where prices hit lows around 66-67 before spiking to 80.",
    "Adjust for sentiment disconnect: Positive sentiment spikes (0.20-0.33) in the history do not trigger immediate upward price corrections in the model; the model remains stuck in a flat, low-volatility trajectory while true prices exhibit high-amplitude swings.",
    "Reduce forecast horizon bias: The model exhibits a persistent upward drift in its 30-day forecast (e.g., 75.09 to 75.90) regardless of the actual downward trend in the first half of the period; forecasts must be adjusted downward by 5-8 EUR for the first 10 days when history shows a recent peak followed by a sharp decline.",
    "Account for overshoot reversal: After the model predicts a slow recovery, true outcomes show a rapid overshoot (peaking at 80.24) followed by a sharp drop; the model fails to predict this secondary spike and subsequent correction, requiring a rule to anticipate a 'double-dip' or 'spike-and-crash' pattern rather than a linear trend.",
    "Invert the volatility assumption: The model assumes a stable, low-variance environment; correction rules must introduce a volatility multiplier to the forecast range, specifically widening the error bands to accommodate the observed 15 EUR swing (66 to 80) that the model completely misses."
  ]
}
